[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Reading/writing tklib geo/box files}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require map::slippy 0.8]
[require snit]
[require map::box::file [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides commands to read and write Tklib [term geo/box] files. The format of these
files is described in section [sectref {File format}], the value returned by [cmd {map box file read}]
and accepted by [cmd {map box file write}] in section [sectref {geo/box value}].

[para] Packages using these files are [package map::box::store::fs], which loads all
[term geo/box] files of a directory, and through it [package map::box::store::memory].

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd {::map box file read}] [arg path]]

Reads the [term geo/box] file [arg path] and returns its contents as a [term geo/box] value, see
section [sectref {geo/box value}].

[para] If the file cannot be opened, or is not a valid [term geo/box] file, the result is the empty
string. No error is thrown in these cases.

[para] If the file does not specify any name, the box gets a single name, the name of the file
without directory and extension. For example, an unnamed box in file [file /data/campus.box] is
named [const campus].

[call [cmd {::map box file write}] [arg path] [arg box]]

Writes the [term geo/box] value [arg box] (see section [sectref {geo/box value}]) to the file
[arg path], replacing any previous contents. The names are written in dictionary order, with
duplicates removed. The coordinates are written in the order given, and are not validated.

[para] The result of the command is the empty string. Failure to open the file is reported as an
error.

[list_end]

[section {geo/box value}]

A [term geo/box] is a rectangle on the map, bounded by two parallels and two meridians, together
with a list of names. It is represented by a Tcl dictionary with the following keys:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const names]]
A list of strings, the names of the box. Several names can be specified, e.g. aliases or names in
different languages.

[def [const geo]]
The box, a list of four numbers in degrees: the minimal latitude, the minimal longitude, the maximal
latitude, and the maximal longitude, in this order. This is the geographical bounding box
([term geobox]) of [package map::slippy].
[list_end]

For example:

[example {names {{Stanley Park}} geo {49.2897 -123.1572 49.3149 -123.1176}}]

[section {File format}]

A [term geo/box] file is a text file, read line by line:

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Leading and trailing whitespace of each line is ignored.

[item] Empty lines are ignored.

[item] Lines starting with [const #] or [const //] are comments, and ignored.

[item] The first line which is neither empty nor a comment must be the magic line
[const tklib/geo/box], identifying the file.

[item] Every other line which is not a number is a name of the box. There may be any number of
names, including none. A name cannot look like a floating point number, it would be read as a
coordinate.

[item] Exactly four lines must be numbers, the coordinates of the box: the minimal latitude, the
minimal longitude, the maximal latitude, and the maximal longitude, in this order. Latitudes must
be in the range -90 to 90, longitudes in the range -180 to 180 degrees. The reader does not check
that the minimum is not greater than the maximum.

[list_end]

[para] By convention the names are written after the magic line and before the coordinates, and
files use the extension [file .box]. This is also the default file pattern of
[package map::box::store::fs].

[para] For example:

[example {tklib/geo/box
Stanley Park
# south-west corner
49.2897
-123.1572
# north-east corner
49.3149
-123.1176}]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require map::box::file

map box file write stanley.box {
    names {{Stanley Park}}
    geo   {49.2897 -123.1572 49.3149 -123.1176}
}

set box [lb]map box file read stanley.box[rb]
lassign [lb]dict get $box geo[rb] latmin lonmin latmax lonmax
puts "$latmin $lonmin - $latmax $lonmax"
[example_end]

